        "heading": "One Brand, Many Products",
        "content": "### Quick Glossary: Every Acronym Before We Start\n\nIf you are new to the Microsoft security stack, here is every product name and acronym used in this lesson, defined once up front so nothing below assumes prior knowledge:\n\n- **MDE** — Microsoft Defender for Endpoint, protecting workstations and servers (EDR).\n- **MDO** — Microsoft Defender for Office 365, protecting email and collaboration tools.\n- **MDI** — Microsoft Defender for Identity, protecting on-premises Active Directory.\n- **MDCA** — Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps, a Cloud Access Security Broker (CASB) for SaaS usage.\n- **Entra ID** — Microsoft's cloud identity platform, formerly named Azure Active Directory (Azure AD).\n- **Microsoft Sentinel** — Microsoft's cloud-native SIEM (Security Information and Event Management), formerly named Azure Sentinel.\n- **Microsoft Purview** — Microsoft's data governance and protection platform, covering DLP (Data Loss Prevention) among other capabilities.\n\nKeep this list in mind — the rest of the lesson uses these names constantly and builds directly on exactly what each one covers.\n\nThe single most important fact to internalize about Microsoft's security portfolio is that 'Defender' is a brand applied to five separate products, each protecting a different part of the environment, and each with its own distinct telemetry and its own console history before they were unified under one portal. Confusing them is not a minor vocabulary slip — it leads analysts to look for evidence in the wrong product entirely, or to assume a license they do not have covers a capability it never included.\n\nFive distinct products share the Defender name, each protecting a different part of the environment:\n\n- **Defender for Endpoint (MDE)** — protects workstations and servers. This is the EDR product covered in depth elsewhere on this platform, generating process, network, logon, file, and registry telemetry from an agent installed on each machine.\n- **Defender for Office 365 (MDO)** — protects email and collaboration tools. It scans inbound mail for phishing and malicious attachments, follows links through Safe Links, detonates attachments in a sandbox through Safe Attachments, and tracks mailbox rule changes that are often the first sign of a compromised account.\n- **Defender for Identity (MDI)** — protects on-premises Active Directory specifically. It watches for Kerberoasting, DCSync, and other AD-native attacks by monitoring domain controller traffic directly, filling a gap the other Defender products cannot, since none of them have native visibility into AD protocol-level abuse.\n- **Defender for Cloud Apps (MDCA)** — Microsoft's Cloud Access Security Broker (CASB). It gives visibility into which SaaS applications the organization actually uses (including unsanctioned 'shadow IT' apps discovered from network traffic), enforces policies on that usage, and flags risky OAuth app consents.\n- **Defender for Cloud** — a different animal again: a cloud security posture and workload protection product covering Azure, AWS, and GCP infrastructure itself, closer in spirit to a CSPM tool than to an endpoint or email product. It is easily confused with Defender for Cloud Apps despite protecting an almost entirely different thing.\n\n**Defender XDR** (formerly Microsoft 365 Defender — the rename matters because both names appear constantly in real documentation and tickets) is not a sixth separate telemetry source; it is the unifying layer that sits above Endpoint, Office 365, Identity, and Cloud Apps, correlating alerts from all four into a single incident when an attack chain spans multiple domains — a phishing email caught by MDO, followed by a compromised account flagged by Defender for Cloud Apps, followed by lateral movement flagged by MDI and MDE. This correlation is exactly what turns four separate, seemingly unrelated alerts into one coherent incident timeline an analyst can investigate as a single story, rather than four disconnected tickets nobody thinks to connect.\n\nThe other renaming every analyst needs to know cold: what used to be called **Azure Active Directory (Azure AD)** is now **Entra ID**, Microsoft's identity and access management product, covered in depth later in this lesson. The underlying service and its logs did not fundamentally change — the product was renamed as part of a broader rebrand of Microsoft's identity and network access portfolio under the Entra name — but 'Azure AD sign-in logs' and 'Entra ID sign-in logs' refer to the exact same data, and an analyst reading older runbooks, forum posts, or a colleague's habitual phrasing needs to recognize both names as the same thing without hesitation.",
