        "heading": "XDR vs SIEM: Defender XDR and Microsoft Sentinel",
        "content": "Defender XDR and Microsoft Sentinel solve related but genuinely different problems, and understanding the difference is what lets an analyst explain — correctly, not by rote — why so many organizations run both rather than treating one as a replacement for the other.\n\n**Defender XDR is an XDR: extended detection and response, purpose-built around Microsoft's own telemetry.** It is deep but narrow by design — it knows Endpoint, Office 365, Identity, and Cloud Apps telemetry intimately, applies Microsoft's own correlation engine to link related alerts into incidents automatically, and ships with a large library of built-in detections tuned specifically for those data sources with essentially no configuration burden on the customer. Its strength is exactly this depth: because it was built by the same vendor that produces the underlying products, it understands the semantics of a mailbox rule change or a Kerberoasting attempt natively, with correlation logic that a generic SIEM would have to be manually taught.\n\n**Microsoft Sentinel is a SIEM: a cloud-native security information and event management platform, and its defining trait is breadth, not depth in any one vendor's telemetry.** Sentinel ingests logs from essentially anything through **Data Connectors** — not just the Microsoft Defender family, but third-party firewalls, on-premises applications, other cloud providers' audit logs, SaaS applications with no Microsoft affiliation at all, and custom log sources an organization builds itself.\n\nWhere Defender XDR's detection logic comes largely pre-built, Sentinel expects the organization to author **Analytics Rules** — KQL queries that run on a schedule and generate alerts when they match — giving far more flexibility to detect organization-specific threats across an organization-specific mix of technology, at the cost of requiring real detection-engineering effort that Defender XDR mostly does not.\n\nSentinel also supports **Playbooks**, built on Azure Logic Apps, which automate response actions — disabling a user account, blocking an IP at the firewall, opening a ticket — triggered directly from a matched Analytics Rule, giving Sentinel a SOAR-like automation layer that sits on top of its detection and correlation capability.\n\nThe comparison in short:\n\n- **Category** — Defender XDR is an XDR; Microsoft Sentinel is a cloud-native SIEM.\n- **Telemetry scope** — Defender XDR sees only Microsoft-native sources (Endpoint, Office 365, Identity, Cloud Apps); Sentinel ingests anything via Data Connectors, including third-party firewalls, other clouds, custom sources, and Defender XDR's own incidents.\n- **Detection content** — Defender XDR ships pre-built detections with low configuration effort; Sentinel relies on Analytics Rules that you author yourself in KQL.\n- **Correlation** — Defender XDR correlates automatically and natively; Sentinel's correlation depends on the rules you build.\n- **Automation** — Defender XDR uses Automated Investigation and Response (AIR); Sentinel uses Playbooks built on Azure Logic Apps.\n\n### Why many organizations run both rather than choosing one\n\nAn organization that is Microsoft-centric — Windows endpoints, Microsoft 365, on-prem AD, Azure infrastructure — gets excellent, low-effort coverage of that specific footprint from Defender XDR alone. But almost no real organization is purely Microsoft-centric: there is a firewall from another vendor, a line-of-business application with its own logs, perhaps AWS or GCP infrastructure alongside Azure, and none of that non-Microsoft telemetry has any way into Defender XDR at all.\n\nSentinel closes that gap by ingesting everything Defender XDR cannot see, and critically, Sentinel has a native Data Connector for Defender XDR itself, meaning an organization can forward all of Defender XDR's already-correlated incidents into Sentinel and get a single pane of glass across the entire environment — Microsoft-native and third-party alike — without losing any of Defender XDR's built-in correlation quality in the process.\n\nThe practical rule of thumb: Defender XDR for deep, low-effort coverage of the Microsoft footprint; Sentinel for organization-wide visibility, custom detection engineering, and SOAR-style automated response, with the two typically wired together rather than run as competing alternatives."
