      {
        "heading": "Worked Example: One Incident, Four Products",
        "content": "Everything above is easier to internalize with one concrete, followable example: a single Defender XDR incident born from a phishing email, read the way an analyst actually reads it — starting from the raw hunting query that first surfaced it, through to the correlated incident record itself.\n\n### Step 1 — Hunt the phishing email in Advanced Hunting\n\nSuppose a report comes in that several users received a suspicious invoice email. The first move is a KQL query against the EmailEvents table — the Advanced Hunting table that records every message Defender for Office 365 processed, with columns for the sender, recipient, subject, and Microsoft's own delivery verdict:\n\n```\nEmailEvents\n| where Timestamp > ago(24h)\n| where ThreatTypes has 'Phish'\n| where SenderFromAddress contains 'invoice'\n| project Timestamp, SenderFromAddress, RecipientEmailAddress, Subject, ThreatTypes, DeliveryAction\n```\n\nRead it line by line: filter to the last day, filter to messages MDO already flagged as phishing in ThreatTypes, narrow to senders whose address contains 'invoice' since that matches the reported lure, and project only the columns that answer 'who sent it, who received it, and what did Defender do with it.' DeliveryAction shows whether the message was blocked, quarantined, or — the concerning case — delivered anyway because a rule or the user's own settings overrode the verdict.\n\n### Step 2 — See it as one correlated Defender XDR incident\n\nOn its own, that query only shows email events. But if a user who received the message went on to approve a suspicious app consent, and that app then triggered activity Defender for Endpoint also flagged, Defender XDR's correlation engine — not the analyst — links all of it into a single incident record. A simplified version of what that incident looks like through the Microsoft Graph Security API:\n\n```\n{\n  \"id\": \"da637812-incident-4471\",\n  \"displayName\": \"Multi-stage incident: phishing lure to endpoint activity\",\n  \"severity\": \"high\",\n  \"status\": \"active\",\n  \"classification\": \"truePositive\",\n  \"determination\": \"phishing\",\n  \"createdDateTime\": \"2026-08-15T09:14:02Z\",\n  \"lastUpdateDateTime\": \"2026-08-15T10:02:11Z\",\n  \"alerts\": [\n    { \"serviceSource\": \"microsoftDefenderForOffice365\", \"title\": \"Phishing email delivered to mailbox\" },\n    { \"serviceSource\": \"microsoftDefenderForCloudApps\", \"title\": \"Suspicious OAuth app consent\" },\n    { \"serviceSource\": \"microsoftDefenderForEndpoint\", \"title\": \"Suspicious PowerShell command line\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotice what each field actually tells you. severity and classification are Microsoft's own assessment, already applied — a classification of truePositive means an analyst or automation already confirmed this was real, not a false alarm still awaiting review. Each entry in the alerts array carries a serviceSource value, and that field is precisely the mechanism that answers the question the first section of this lesson raised: which Defender product actually generated this piece of evidence. Reading the three alerts in order — Office 365, then Cloud Apps, then Endpoint — is reading the attack chain itself: a phishing email led to a risky app consent, which led to activity on a device.\n\n### Step 3 — Why this matters for triage\n\nAn analyst opening this incident should not treat the three alerts as three separate tickets. The serviceSource values tell you which product to go pivot into for more detail on each stage — back into EmailEvents for the delivery details already queried above, into Defender for Cloud Apps' activity log for exactly which permissions the OAuth app requested, and into Defender for Endpoint's process tree for what the PowerShell command line actually did. The incident record is the map; the individual product consoles are still where you go to read the fine print."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Identity: Entra ID",
        "content": "Entra ID (formerly Azure AD) is Microsoft's cloud identity and access management platform, and its logs are frequently the very first place an investigation touches when an incident might involve a compromised account. A distinction that consistently confuses analysts moving into this platform is the difference between **Sign-in logs** and **Audit logs** — two separate log streams recording fundamentally different categories of activity, and confusing them means looking in the wrong place for the evidence an investigation needs.\n\n**Sign-in logs record authentication events** — every attempt by a user, service principal, or managed identity to authenticate, whether it succeeded or failed, including the location the sign-in originated from, the device involved, the application being signed into, the authentication method used, and whether Conditional Access policies applied and what they decided. If the question is 'did this user actually log in, from where, on what device, and was MFA satisfied,' the sign-in log is the source.\n\n**Audit logs record configuration and directory changes** — a new user created, a role assigned, an application registered, a Conditional Access policy modified, a group membership changed. If the question is 'what changed in the directory, and who changed it,' the audit log is the source, and it is a fundamentally different question than 'who logged in.'\n\nAn analyst investigating account compromise typically needs both: the sign-in log to establish how and when an attacker authenticated, and the audit log to see what that attacker then did with the access — creating a new application registration for persistence, for instance, is an audit log event, not a sign-in log event, even though it happened in the same session.\n\n**Conditional Access** is Entra ID's policy engine, enforcing rules like 'require MFA for sign-ins from outside the corporate network' or 'block sign-in entirely from non-compliant devices' at the moment of authentication. When a Conditional Access policy blocks or challenges a sign-in, that decision appears directly in the sign-in log as part of the event, letting an analyst see not just that a sign-in occurred but exactly which policy evaluated it and what that policy decided — a denied sign-in with a Conditional Access policy listed as the reason is a very different finding than a denied sign-in due to a simple wrong password.\n\n**Identity Protection** layers risk scoring on top of this raw telemetry, producing two related but distinct risk signals that are easy to conflate. **Sign-in risk** scores a specific authentication attempt — is this particular sign-in unusual given its location, device, and behavior pattern, evaluated fresh each time.\n\n**User risk** scores the account itself over time — has this identity shown a pattern consistent with compromise across multiple signals, such as credentials appearing in a leaked-credential feed or a string of anomalous sign-ins, and it persists as a standing risk level on the account until it is investigated and explicitly remediated or dismissed.\n\nA single risky sign-in can occur without necessarily elevating the account's overall user risk; conversely, a user's risk level can remain elevated even after any single sign-in looks unremarkable, because the risk reflects the account's pattern, not just its most recent login.\n\n**MFA fatigue** is a real-world scenario tying all of this together: an attacker who already has valid stolen credentials but cannot complete authentication without an MFA approval repeatedly triggers push notifications to the legitimate user's phone, hoping the user approves one out of irritation, habit, or confusion, rather than out of any belief the request is legitimate.\n\nIn the sign-in log, this appears as a burst of MFA prompts to the same user in a short window, frequently followed by exactly one approval — a pattern an analyst should recognize immediately and treat as a compromise indicator, not routine login friction, and it is exactly the kind of pattern a KQL query summarizing sign-in attempts per user per short time window is well suited to surface at scale.",
        "codeExample": "// Entra ID — two log streams, two different questions\n//\n// SIGN-IN LOGS                           AUDIT LOGS\n// answers: \"did this user authenticate,   answers: \"what changed in the\n// from where, on what device, was MFA     directory, and who changed it?\"\n// satisfied, what did Conditional         (new user, role assignment, app\n// Access decide?\"                         registration, CA policy edit)\n//\n// Investigating account compromise needs BOTH:\n//   sign-in log  -> how/when the attacker got in\n//   audit log    -> what the attacker did with the access afterward\n//\n// Identity Protection risk signals — do not conflate these two:\n//   Sign-in risk  -> scores ONE authentication attempt, evaluated fresh\n//   User risk     -> scores the ACCOUNT over time, persists until remediated\n//\n// MFA FATIGUE pattern in sign-in logs:\n//   burst of MFA push prompts to the same user, short window,\n//   followed by exactly one approval -> treat as compromise, not friction\n//\n// KQL sketch to surface it:\nSigninLogs\n| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)\n| summarize PromptCount = count() by UserPrincipalName\n| where PromptCount > 5"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Data and Posture: Purview and Secure Score",
        "content": "The last piece of the Microsoft stack a working analyst needs is the pair of tools that shift attention from 'what is happening right now' to 'what data do we have, and how well-configured are our controls' — **Microsoft Purview** for data protection and **Secure Score** for posture measurement, and both come with an important caveat about what their numbers actually mean.\n\n**Microsoft Purview** is Microsoft's unified data governance and protection platform, and its DLP (Data Loss Prevention) capability is what most directly intersects with day-to-day SOC work. Purview DLP policies scan content — email, files in SharePoint and OneDrive, Teams messages, endpoint activity — for **sensitive information types**: pre-built or custom pattern definitions covering things like credit card numbers, national ID numbers, source code patterns, or health record identifiers, each with a defined detection pattern and confidence level.\n\nWhen a policy matches, Purview can take actions ranging from silently logging the event, to warning the user with a policy tip before they send a message, to outright blocking the action entirely, depending on how the policy is configured.\n\nA DLP incident an analyst reviews typically shows:\n\n- Which sensitive information type matched.\n- The confidence level of that match.\n- Which policy triggered.\n- What user and location were involved.\n- What action was taken.\n\nFor example: a user attempting to email a spreadsheet externally that Purview identified as containing a high-confidence match for a national ID number pattern was blocked automatically before the message left the tenant. Reading a Purview DLP alert correctly means checking not just that something matched, but the confidence level and the specific policy rule, since low-confidence matches on common patterns generate real false positives that a hasty analyst can escalate unnecessarily.\n\n**Secure Score** is a different kind of number entirely, and the distinction matters enough to state explicitly: it is a posture metric, not a security guarantee. Secure Score aggregates the organization's adoption of a large set of Microsoft-recommended security controls — MFA enforcement coverage, whether legacy authentication protocols are disabled, whether Conditional Access policies meet certain baselines, patch compliance levels, and dozens of similar recommendations — into a single percentage score, with specific, actionable recommendations attached to each point of improvement. This makes it a genuinely useful tool for prioritizing where a security team should invest effort next, and for demonstrating measurable progress to leadership over time.\n\n**The caveat every analyst should be able to explain clearly: a high Secure Score measures how many recommended controls have been adopted, not how secure the organization actually is.** An organization can score well while still suffering a breach through a technique entirely outside what Secure Score measures, such as:\n\n- A zero-day vulnerability.\n- A sophisticated social-engineering attack against a well-configured but ultimately human user.\n- A misconfiguration in a system Secure Score does not evaluate at all.\n\nConversely, a lower score does not automatically mean an organization is actively being breached; it means specific, known-good controls have not yet been adopted. Presenting Secure Score to leadership as 'proof we are secure' rather than 'proof of which recommended controls we have adopted so far' is a common and consequential misstatement, and a security-literate analyst should be the one correcting that framing whenever it comes up, rather than reinforcing it."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Managing the Devices Themselves: Microsoft Intune and Conditional Access",
        "content": "Everything covered so far in this lesson assumes an identity is trying to reach data or an application, and that Entra ID and Conditional Access decide whether to let it through. But Conditional Access has an entire category of signal it cannot evaluate at all unless one more product is in play: whether the device itself is trustworthy. That product is **Microsoft Intune**, Microsoft's unified endpoint management platform, and understanding what it actually contributes closes a real gap in how analysts reason about access decisions.\n\n### MDM and MAM: two different promises about a device\n\nIntune makes two genuinely different kinds of promise, and mixing them up leads to wrong assumptions about what an organization can actually see or enforce on a given device.\n\n- **MDM (Mobile Device Management)** — full enrollment. The organization manages the entire device: it can push configuration profiles, enforce disk encryption, require a passcode, remotely wipe the device, and continuously evaluate compliance against a defined policy (patch level, encryption state, jailbreak/root detection, antivirus status). This is the model for corporate-owned laptops and phones.\n- **MAM (Mobile Application Management)** — app-level control without owning the device. On a personal phone an employee will never enroll in full MDM, Intune can still wrap just the corporate apps (Outlook, Teams) in an **App Protection Policy**: require a PIN to open the app, block copy-paste of corporate data into personal apps, and remotely wipe only the corporate app's data, leaving personal photos and apps untouched. This is the standard model for BYOD (bring your own device).\n\n### How this reaches Conditional Access\n\nIntune evaluates each enrolled device against a **compliance policy** — a defined bar covering things like minimum OS version, disk encryption enabled, and no known high-severity vulnerabilities reported by Defender for Endpoint. The result is a compliance state (compliant or noncompliant) reported back to Entra ID, and that state becomes a condition a Conditional Access policy can require, alongside or instead of requiring MFA.\n\nThis is the mechanism behind a very common real policy: require MFA and a compliant device for all administrator sign-ins. An administrator with the right password and the right MFA code, on a personal laptop that has never enrolled in Intune, still gets blocked, because the device itself never reported compliant.\n\nThe important caveat for BYOD: an unenrolled personal device cannot report a compliance state at all, since there is nothing evaluating it. A Conditional Access policy that requires a compliant device therefore cannot be satisfied by an unmanaged personal machine, which is precisely why organizations that allow BYOD typically fall back to the MAM-based app protection policy as the enforcement point on those devices instead.\n\n### Reading it as an analyst\n\nWhen a sign-in is denied and the sign-in log names a Conditional Access policy requiring device compliance as the reason, the investigative pivot is into Intune's device compliance report for that device, not back into the sign-in log itself, because the sign-in log will tell you a policy blocked it but Intune is where you find out which specific compliance check the device failed.",
        "codeExample": "// Conditional Access policy requiring MFA AND a compliant device for admins\n{\n  \"displayName\": \"Require compliant device for admin roles\",\n  \"conditions\": { \"users\": { \"includeRoles\": [\"Global Administrator\", \"Security Administrator\"] } },\n  \"grantControls\": {\n    \"operator\": \"AND\",\n    \"builtInControls\": [\"mfa\", \"compliantDevice\"]\n  }\n}\n\n// Intune device compliance report -- why THIS device failed\n{\n  \"deviceName\": \"LAPTOP-J-KAPLAN\",\n  \"complianceState\": \"noncompliant\",\n  \"managementType\": \"mdm\",\n  \"failedSettings\": [\"BitLocker disk encryption not enabled\", \"OS build below minimum required version\"]\n}\n\n// Analyst pivot: a sign-in denial citing \"compliantDevice\" in the CA policy\n// name means go check Intune's device compliance report next -- the\n// sign-in log only tells you THAT it failed, not WHICH check failed."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Just-in-Time Privilege: Microsoft Entra Privileged Identity Management (PIM)",
        "content": "Every product covered so far assumes a role, once assigned, simply exists — a Global Administrator is a Global Administrator until someone removes the role. That assumption is exactly the weakness **Microsoft Entra Privileged Identity Management (PIM)** exists to close, and it is one of the most consequential controls in the entire Microsoft stack for limiting what a compromised admin account can actually do.\n\n### Standing access versus eligible access\n\nWithout PIM, a privileged role assignment is 'active' the moment it is granted and stays active indefinitely. That means a compromised credential for that account is immediately a compromised privileged account, twenty-four hours a day, whether or not the real administrator is doing anything privileged at that moment.\n\nPIM introduces a second state between 'has the role' and 'does not have the role': **eligible**. A user who is eligible for a role does not hold it by default. They must actively **activate** it, for a limited time window defined by policy, before the permissions actually apply. When the window expires, the role is automatically deactivated with no action required — the administrator is simply an ordinary user again until the next time they need to activate it.\n\n### What a PIM policy can require at activation\n\nActivation is not a single click by default. A PIM role policy can require any combination of:\n\n- **Multi-factor authentication**, re-confirmed at the moment of activation, not just at the original sign-in.\n- **A written justification** — a short text field explaining why the role is needed right now, which becomes part of the audit trail.\n- **Approval** from a named approver, turning self-service elevation into a request-and-grant workflow for the most sensitive roles.\n- **A maximum activation duration**, commonly a matter of hours, after which the role automatically lapses.\n\n### Why this matters for detection, not just prevention\n\nPIM's audit history records every activation — who activated which role, when, for how long, with what justification, and whether approval was required and granted. That log is a genuinely powerful detection surface: a Global Administrator role activated at 3am with no prior pattern of night-shift activity, or a role activated without the justification field being filled in on a tenant that requires it, is exactly the kind of anomaly worth a KQL query on its own.\n\nPIM also supports **alerts** on suspicious patterns it recognizes natively, such as roles being assigned outside of PIM entirely (a standing assignment bypassing the eligible/activate model), or the same role being activated repeatedly in a way that suggests it should simply be made standing — which is itself a finding worth investigating, since a role activated every single day is arguably not being time-bound in practice even if it is time-bound on paper.\n\n### The caveat\n\nPIM reduces the *window* of exposure, not the *ceiling* of what a compromised session can do. An attacker who compromises an account during an active, already-elevated PIM session inherits every privilege that session currently holds, for as long as that session lasts. PIM is a control on standing risk over time, not a substitute for the account-level protections — strong authentication, Conditional Access, session monitoring — that keep the account from being compromised at all.",
        "codeExample": "// PIM role: eligible vs active, and what a policy can require\nRole assignment type      What it means\n-----------------------   --------------------------------------------\nEligible                  User CAN request the role; does not hold it\n                          until they activate it\nActive (via PIM)          User currently holds the role, for a\n                          time-bound window set at activation\nActive (standing,         User holds the role permanently -- exactly\noutside PIM)              what PIM exists to eliminate for sensitive roles\n\n// Simplified PIM activation audit record\n{\n  \"activityDisplayName\": \"Add member to role completed (PIM activation)\",\n  \"targetRole\": \"Global Administrator\",\n  \"initiatedBy\": \"d.kaplan@contoso.com\",\n  \"justification\": \"Investigating a failed conditional access deployment\",\n  \"durationRequestedHours\": 4,\n  \"mfaConfirmedAtActivation\": true,\n  \"approvalRequired\": true,\n  \"approvedBy\": \"security-lead@contoso.com\",\n  \"activatedAt\": \"2026-08-20T02:47:11Z\"\n}\n// A 3am activation with no approval on file, or a role assigned as a\n// STANDING assignment outside PIM entirely, is exactly what a\n// KQL query against AuditLogs should be built to surface."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Securing the Cloud Infrastructure Itself: Microsoft Defender for Cloud",
        "content": "The earlier map of the Defender family flagged that Defender for Cloud is easily confused with Defender for Cloud Apps despite protecting an almost entirely different thing, and that confusion is common enough to be worth resolving properly rather than leaving as a footnote. Defender for Cloud Apps is a CASB — it governs how people use SaaS applications. **Defender for Cloud** governs the security of cloud *infrastructure itself* — the virtual machines, storage accounts, containers, databases, and their configuration, across Azure and, through native connectors, AWS and Google Cloud Platform (GCP) as well.\n\n### Two capabilities, not one product doing one thing\n\nDefender for Cloud actually bundles two distinct functions that are worth naming separately, because they answer different questions:\n\n- **CSPM (Cloud Security Posture Management)** — continuously assesses how cloud resources are *configured*, against recommendations like 'storage accounts should not allow public write access' or 'a network security group should not allow unrestricted inbound access on management ports.' This runs agentlessly, simply reading configuration through the cloud provider's own APIs, and produces a **Secure Score for cloud resources** built the same way as the Microsoft 365 Secure Score covered earlier in this lesson — a percentage reflecting how many recommended configuration controls have been adopted, with the same caveat that a high score reflects adopted configuration, not immunity from every possible attack.\n- **CWPP (Cloud Workload Protection Platform)** — actively protects the *running workload*, not just its configuration. Defender for Servers watches for suspicious process activity and brute-force attempts on virtual machines; Defender for Containers watches for anomalous behavior inside running containers and scans container images for known vulnerabilities before deployment; Defender for Storage scans uploaded files for malware. This tier requires an agent or an equivalent runtime integration and is priced separately from the free, foundational CSPM tier.\n\n### Foundational versus paid CSPM\n\nMicrosoft explicitly splits CSPM into two tiers: a **free, foundational** tier that gives basic visibility and Secure Score recommendations to every Azure subscription automatically, and a **paid Defender CSPM** tier that adds attack-path analysis (mapping how a specific misconfiguration could actually be chained by an attacker into a real compromise) and agentless vulnerability scanning for VMs. The free tier is a baseline every organization already has running; the paid tier is what turns a long list of individual recommendations into a prioritized, exploit-path-aware set of fixes.\n\n### Reading a finding\n\nA Defender for Cloud recommendation an analyst reviews typically states the affected resource, the specific control that is not met, a severity, and — for Defender CSPM — whether that gap sits on an active attack path to something sensitive, which is the detail that should move a finding from a routine backlog item to something worth escalating immediately.",
        "codeExample": "// Defender for Cloud finding -- CSPM configuration recommendation\n{\n  \"recommendationDisplayName\": \"Storage accounts should restrict network access\",\n  \"resourceId\": \"/subscriptions/.../storageAccounts/prodinvoices01\",\n  \"severity\": \"High\",\n  \"status\": \"Unhealthy\",\n  \"cloudProvider\": \"AWS\",\n  \"attackPath\": \"Publicly-accessible storage -> contains credentials -> reachable VM with admin role\"\n}\n// The attackPath field is what Defender CSPM (paid) adds over the\n// free foundational tier: it is not just \"this is misconfigured\", it\n// is \"here is the exploitable chain a real attacker could walk.\"\n\n// Defender for Servers (CWPP) alert -- an actively running workload issue,\n// not a configuration finding:\n{\n  \"alertDisplayName\": \"Suspicious process executed on Azure VM\",\n  \"resourceId\": \"/subscriptions/.../virtualMachines/prod-web-03\",\n  \"processName\": \"certutil.exe -urlcache -split -f\",\n  \"mitreTechniques\": [\"T1105\"]\n}"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Watching for Risk From the Inside: Microsoft Purview Insider Risk Management",
        "content": "Purview DLP, covered earlier in this lesson, works by inspecting *content* — does this specific email or file contain a pattern that matches a sensitive information type. It has no concept of a person's behavior over time. **Insider Risk Management**, a separate capability inside the same Purview platform, fills exactly that gap: it correlates *behavioral signals* across many activities and many days to score how risky a specific person's overall pattern looks, independent of whether any single action on its own would ever trigger a DLP rule.\n\n### Policy templates and indicators\n\nInsider Risk Management ships with **policy templates** built around common real-world scenarios, each one built from a defined set of **indicators** — the individual signals the policy watches for. Common template categories include:\n\n- **Data theft by departing users** — a resignation or termination event in HR data, correlated with unusual download, printing, or file-copy activity in the weeks around that date.\n- **Data leaks** — sensitive content shared externally, through email, cloud storage, or removable media, at a volume or pattern that stands out from that user's own history.\n- **Security policy violations** — activity like disabling security tooling, unusual PowerShell use, or repeated attempts to access resources outside a person's normal role.\n\nEvery indicator is **disabled by default** — an organization must deliberately choose which behaviors to watch for, which is a meaningful design choice: it means Insider Risk Management does not passively surveil everything by default, it only evaluates the specific indicator categories a policy owner has explicitly turned on.\n\n### Privacy by design: pseudonymization\n\nBecause this capability necessarily involves scoring individual employees' behavior, Microsoft built a specific privacy control into it: by default, users flagged by a policy appear to the reviewing analyst under a **pseudonym**, not their real name. Only a small, separately role-assigned group of reviewers with the specific permission to do so can reveal the real identity behind a pseudonym, and that reveal action is itself logged. This matters operationally: a Tier 1 SOC analyst is very unlikely to be the role permitted to de-pseudonymize a case, and knowing that in advance avoids a confused escalation when a case shows up under a codename instead of a person's name.\n\n### What happens after an alert\n\nA triggered policy produces an alert with a risk score, which a reviewer can escalate into a formal **case** for deeper investigation, and — where the evidence supports it — into an **eDiscovery** hold to preserve the relevant content for legal or HR action. This is the point where the workflow deliberately leaves purely technical territory: Insider Risk Management surfaces the pattern, but what happens next routinely involves HR and Legal, not the SOC alone, precisely because the subject of the investigation is an employee, not an external attacker.",
        "codeExample": "// Insider Risk Management -- indicator categories (illustrative, template-based)\nTemplate category            Example indicators it correlates\n---------------------------  ---------------------------------------------\nDeparting user data theft    Resignation date (from HR feed) + spike in\n                             downloads/print/copy-to-USB in surrounding weeks\nData leaks                   Large external share/email volume vs. that\n                             user's own historical baseline\nSecurity policy violations   Security tooling disabled, anomalous PowerShell,\n                             repeated access attempts outside normal role\n\n// A pseudonymized alert as a Tier 1 reviewer would actually see it --\n// note the real identity is deliberately withheld:\n{\n  \"policyName\": \"Data theft by departing users\",\n  \"userPseudonym\": \"User8842\",\n  \"riskScore\": \"High\",\n  \"triggeringActivities\": [\"Resignation recorded 11 days prior\", \"47 files copied to USB in past 48h\"],\n  \"deidentificationState\": \"pseudonymized\"\n}\n// Revealing \"User8842\" requires a separate, logged permission most\n// SOC analysts do not hold -- this is a deliberate privacy control,\n// not a bug or a missing feature."
      }
